<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Basses-Pyrénées</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1790">1790</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Pyrénées-Atlantiques</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Basses-Pyrénées was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> created in 1790 from Béarn, Lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/labourd">Labourd</a> and Soule, with Pau as its capital. It covered the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> and the French Basque Country on the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. In 1969 it was renamed Pyrénées-Atlantiques.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/basses-pyrenees.jpg" alt="Basses-Pyrénées" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tangopaso · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint-P%C3%A9e-sur-Nivelle_(lac),_4.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Pyrénées-Atlantiques, named after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> mountain range and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a>, lies in the south-west corner of metropolitan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, in the region of Nouvelle-Aquitaine. It covers the French Basque Country and the Béarn, and its prefecture is Pau. It is bordered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> of Landes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hautes-pyrenees">Hautes-Pyrénées</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gers">Gers</a>, by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-biscay">Bay of Biscay</a> to the west and by the Spanish provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huesca">Huesca</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gipuzkoa">Gipuzkoa</a> to the south. The Pyrenees cross it from east to west, and the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> follows the chain. Its highest point is the Pic Palas, in the Balaïtous massif, at 2,974 m.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Originally named Basses-Pyrénées, it was one of the 83 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> created during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>, on 4 March 1790. It comprised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/labourd">Labourd</a> and Soule, in the provinces of Guyenne and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a>, as well as Lower Navarre, still at least nominally part of the Kingdom of Navarre, and the Béarn. Six districts were set up, with the prefecture first at Navarrenx before it moved to Pau on 14 October 1790.</p><p>The reform ended native institutions and laws. All the Basque estates representatives from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/labourd">Labourd</a> opposed the new layout, the representatives of Lower Navarre refused to vote in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on the ground that they were not part of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and those of Soule voted against. The brothers Garat, representing Labourd, voted yes, thinking it would give them a say in later decisions. In 1800 the six districts were replaced by five arrondissements: Pau, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orthez">Orthez</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonne">Bayonne</a>, Mauléon and Oloron. In 1926 a reform under the government of Raymond Poincaré abolished those of Orthez and Mauléon, leaving the three that remain.</p><p>In the 1950s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> formed part of the newly created region of Aquitaine. In 1969 it was renamed Pyrénées-Atlantiques, because its inhabitants found Basses-Pyrénées pejorative compared with the name of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hautes-pyrenees">Hautes-Pyrénées</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The west is mainly inhabited by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basques">Basques</a> and the east by the Béarnais, who have shared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> since the Revolution; from 1790 to 1969 the inhabitants were called Bas-Pyrénéens. Gascon, a dialect of Occitan, and Basque, a language isolate, are indigenous in their districts, but French is the predominant native language. Pau and the urban area of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonne">Bayonne</a>, Anglet and Biarritz are the two main urban concentrations. The department has 706,564 inhabitants in 2023 and is known for its tourism and its links with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. Its coat of arms combines those of Lower Navarre, Béarn, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/labourd">Labourd</a> and Soule.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pyrénées-Atlantiques” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Revolution abolished the Gramonts' privileges, and in 1790 Bidache was incorporated into France and the new department of <b>Basses-Pyrénées</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-bidache">Principality of Bidache</a></li></ul></section>
